BASELINE on a clean tree: real 126, insns T204/O204, multiset 0t, and `wf_word_diff.py game/sys/memcard drawMemCardMessage` = 204 insns, DIFFERING WORDS = 61 - reproducing MC's recorded 61 exactly, so MC's state assumptions hold and were verified rather than inherited.

THE CORRESPONDENCE, read from `fnasm game/sys/memcard drawMemCardMessage 0x0:0xe0 --diff` and stated as a table because no prior record on this function contains one. Left column is the source object, then TARGET register, then OURS:
  msg / options / count1 / count2 (the four params, copied at 0x14-0x20)  ->  r28 r29 r30 r31  vs  r22 r23 r24 r25
  extra (defined 0x80 `addi rX,r3,4`, used 0x84 cmplwi and 0x8c lwz)      ->  r22            vs  r26
  quad  (defined 0xcc `mr rX,r3` after MBNewTempQuad)                     ->  r25            vs  r28
  blit  (defined 0xc0 `mr rX,r3` after MBNewBlit)                         ->  r26            vs  r29
  savedBusy (defined 0x34 `lwz rX,gGameBusy`)                             ->  r27            vs  r30
  win (0x2c) -> r20 in BOTH; the constant 1 -> r21 in BOTH.
Both streams use exactly ten of r20-r31 and both open with `stmw r20,136(r1)`; the two unused registers are r23,r24 in the target and r27,r31 in ours. THE DECISIVE OBSERVATION: the locals' RELATIVE order is ALREADY IDENTICAL in both builds (savedBusy > blit > quad > ... > extra, descending). What differs is a single BLOCK ROTATION - the target colours the four parameters ABOVE the locals (r28-r31, i.e. allocated first, descending from r31), and we colour them BELOW (r22-r25). All 61 differing words are that one rotation.

D1 (REVERTED, NEUTRAL-REARRANGED - object bytes CHANGED, real flat at 126). Swap the two initialised declarations so the target's zeroing ORDER is reproduced:
    void* blit = 0;
    void* extra = 0;
MEASURED: the emission order at 0x24/0x28 SWAPPED as predicted - our 0x24 became `li r29,0` (blit) and 0x28 `li r26,0` (extra), matching the target's semantic order (target zeroes blit at 0x24 and extra at 0x28). But EVERY register was unchanged: extra still r26, blit still r29, quad still r28. So declaration order among the initialised locals schedules the stores and does NOT renumber. Not kept: it is score-neutral and it destroys a coincidental byte match (baseline's 0x24 `li r26,0` happened to equal the target's `li r26,0` because extra-in-ours and blit-in-target share r26).

D2 (REVERTED, NEUTRAL-IDENTICAL - object bytes UNCHANGED vs D1, i.e. the edit folded away before codegen). Drop both declaration initialisers and assign in the body instead, at the position the target emits them:
    void* blit;
    void* extra;
    ...
    blit = 0;
    extra = 0;
    win = gWinGlobals;
MEASURED byte-identical to D1. This is CATEGORICAL, not a weak negative: MWCC canonicalises a declaration initialiser and a leading body assignment to the same IR, so the initialiser-position axis cannot reach the allocation decision AT ALL and no respelling of it can. It also disposes of the obvious reading of the SP law here - initialiser PRESENCE is not what is separating the two groups in this function.

D3 (REVERTED, REGRESSED real 126 -> 128, multiset still 0t/204-204). Move the uninitialised `quad` to the head of the declaration list:
    void* quad;
    void* win;
    void* savedBusy;
    void* extra = 0;
    void* blit = 0;
MEASURED real 126 -> 128; wf_word_diff on that object read 62 words against HEAD's 61. This is the probe that proves the lever is LIVE for colouring - D1 was colour-neutral by luck, not because declaration order is inert - but it moves the assignment only LOCALLY and in the wrong direction, and no permutation of the local list can express the required change, because the locals are already in the correct relative order and what must move is the parameter block relative to them.
